{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And you shall make a parapet</b> — the commandment of the parapet is new, or it expounds \"you shall not stand idly by the blood of your neighbor\" (Leviticus 19:16). But the commandment of kil'ayim (diverse kinds) is expounding, for He already said (ibid. v. 19) \"your field you shall not sow with two kinds of seed.\" Now in the plain meaning of that verse, it refers to any place that is sown, and here He adds that if one sows the vineyard with kil'ayim, the seed that was sown and the produce of the vineyard become consecrated (תקדש) — meaning that they become forbidden for benefit like consecrated property (הקדש). And the meaning of פן (pen, \"lest\") is like כי תקדש (ki tikdash, \"when it is consecrated\"), and similarly \"and from the entrance of the Tent of Meeting you shall not go out, פן (lest) you die\" (Leviticus 10:7), and likewise \"and you shall not touch it, פן (lest) you die\" (Genesis 3:3). And it is possible that its meaning is like פן ינכרו צרימו פן יאמרו ידינו רמה (Deuteronomy 32:27), in the sense of perhaps (שמא), meaning: you shall not sow that kil'ayim lest it sprout and all of it become forbidden to you — for they do not become forbidden except as we learned (Kil'ayim 7:7): produce becomes consecrated once it takes root, and grapes once they form like a white seed-pearl (כפול הלבן)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The Hebrew כפול הלבן is a technical term from Kil'ayim 7:7 referring to the early stage of grape formation when the blossom cap falls off; rendered literally as 'a white seed-pearl' following the Mishnaic idiom."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:53:23.309337003Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}